INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving technologist explains that increasing the number of HEALD SHAFTS used for a plain weave design BEYOND the minimum required (2 shafts for basic plain weave) — e.g., using 4 shafts where ends are distributed across all 4 in a specific rotating pattern — is sometimes done deliberately even though it produces the IDENTICAL plain weave interlacing pattern. What practical benefit does this ''shaft-sharing'' technique offer?', '["Using more shafts than structurally necessary for plain weave provides no benefit whatsoever and is always a wasteful, pointless practice with zero technical justification","Distributing warp ends across MORE shafts (even for a structurally simple weave) reduces the number of ends each INDIVIDUAL shaft must carry and cycle through its up/down motion, which reduces the mechanical stress/wear concentrated on any single shaft and can reduce the frequency of heald-eye-related warp abrasion at any one specific shaft — this is a deliberate machine-longevity/warp-protection technique, not a structural requirement, similar in spirit to distributing load across multiple resources rather than concentrating it","Using more shafts than necessary always REDUCES loom speed capability with zero offsetting benefit of any kind, making this technique universally counterproductive in every weaving scenario","Shaft count has no relationship to mechanical wear, warp abrasion, or any practical weaving consideration, making this entire discussed benefit technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'Distributing warp ends across MORE shafts (even for a structurally simple weave) reduces the number of ends each INDIVIDUAL shaft must carry and cycle through its up/down motion, which reduces the mechanical stress/wear concentrated on any single shaft and can reduce the frequency of heald-eye-related warp abrasion at any one specific shaft — this is a deliberate machine-longevity/warp-protection technique, not a structural requirement, similar in spirit to distributing load across multiple resources rather than concentrating it', 'This is a genuine, somewhat counterintuitive practical weaving technique — even though plain weave only structurally REQUIRES 2 shafts, distributing the total warp end count across MORE shafts (with each shaft carrying proportionally FEWER ends) reduces the mechanical load/wear concentrated on any single shaft''s healds, and can reduce heald-eye-related warp abrasion frequency at any one location — this is purely a mechanical load-distribution/longevity benefit, not a structural design requirement, illustrating that shaft usage decisions in weaving aren''t always purely about achieving a specific weave PATTERN, but can also address machine wear and warp protection considerations.', 3, '{"shaft-sharing-load-distribution"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric technologist compares two approaches to increasing a woven fabric''s tear strength (beyond the earlier weave-structure/float-length discussion): using a THICKER single yarn at the SAME EPI/PPI, versus using the SAME yarn count but adding periodic reinforcement threads (e.g., a heavier yarn inserted every 10th end, a ''rip-stop'' technique). What is the KEY functional difference in HOW these two approaches improve tear resistance?', '["Both approaches improve tear resistance through mathematically identical mechanisms, making the distinction between uniformly thicker yarn and periodic rip-stop reinforcement purely cosmetic with zero functional difference","A uniformly thicker yarn increases tear resistance EVERYWHERE in the fabric proportionally (and also increases overall fabric weight/GSM uniformly), while rip-stop-style periodic reinforcement threads create localized ''strong points'' that specifically interrupt and STOP a tear''s propagation once it reaches a reinforced thread — this achieves meaningfully improved tear resistance with a much smaller GSM/weight penalty than uniformly thickening the entire yarn population, since only a small percentage of threads need to be upgraded to interrupt tear propagation","Rip-stop periodic reinforcement always REDUCES fabric weight below the original unreinforced fabric''s weight, an outcome that would defy basic physics regardless of the reinforcement technique used","Tear strength has no relationship to either yarn thickness or reinforcement thread placement, making both described techniques technically irrelevant to actually improving fabric tear resistance"]'::jsonb, 'A uniformly thicker yarn increases tear resistance EVERYWHERE in the fabric proportionally (and also increases overall fabric weight/GSM uniformly), while rip-stop-style periodic reinforcement threads create localized ''strong points'' that specifically interrupt and STOP a tear''s propagation once it reaches a reinforced thread — this achieves meaningfully improved tear resistance with a much smaller GSM/weight penalty than uniformly thickening the entire yarn population, since only a small percentage of threads need to be upgraded to interrupt tear propagation', 'This connects the earlier tear-strength/weave-structure discussion with an efficient, targeted engineering solution — a uniformly thicker yarn improves tear resistance but at the cost of proportionally increasing weight/GSM across the ENTIRE fabric; a rip-stop technique (periodic, strategically-spaced stronger reinforcement threads) instead creates specific ''circuit-breaker'' points that a propagating tear tends to stop at once it reaches one of these reinforced threads — since only a SMALL percentage of total threads need this upgrade to achieve this tear-stopping function, rip-stop achieves a meaningful tear-resistance improvement at a much smaller overall weight penalty than uniformly upgrading every yarn, a genuine engineering-efficiency advantage of targeted reinforcement over blanket material upgrade.', 3, '{"ripstop-vs-uniform-thickening"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving mill''s technical team explains that a loom''s ''warp let-off'' and ''fabric take-up'' systems must be PRECISELY synchronized (not just each individually well-controlled) — if let-off delivers slightly MORE warp than take-up removes over time, what fabric defect results, and why does this require synchronized (not just independently accurate) control?', '["Let-off and take-up operate completely independently with zero need for synchronization, making any described defect from their imbalance technically impossible under normal loom operation","If let-off delivers warp FASTER than take-up removes fabric, EXCESS SLACK warp accumulates in the weaving zone over time, progressively reducing effective warp tension below the intended level — even if BOTH systems are individually well-calibrated to their own target rates, a small persistent RATE MISMATCH between them (not necessarily either system being individually ''wrong'') causes this slack to accumulate progressively, eventually causing shed formation problems, uneven beating, or other tension-related defects — this is precisely why the two systems must be synchronized RELATIVE TO EACH OTHER, not just independently accurate","Excess warp slack from a let-off/take-up rate mismatch always causes the OPPOSITE problem (excessive tension) rather than slack, the reverse of the actual relationship between excess warp delivery and resulting fabric tension","Warp slack accumulation from a let-off/take-up mismatch self-corrects automatically within a few picks with zero need for any synchronized control system, making this entire synchronization concern technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'If let-off delivers warp FASTER than take-up removes fabric, EXCESS SLACK warp accumulates in the weaving zone over time, progressively reducing effective warp tension below the intended level — even if BOTH systems are individually well-calibrated to their own target rates, a small persistent RATE MISMATCH between them (not necessarily either system being individually ''wrong'') causes this slack to accumulate progressively, eventually causing shed formation problems, uneven beating, or other tension-related defects — this is precisely why the two systems must be synchronized RELATIVE TO EACH OTHER, not just independently accurate', 'This is a genuinely important systems-engineering insight distinct from either system''s INDIVIDUAL accuracy — even if let-off and take-up are each individually well-calibrated to their own target delivery/removal rate, a small persistent RATE MISMATCH between the two (one slightly faster than intended relative to the other) causes warp slack to accumulate PROGRESSIVELY over time (not a one-time error, but a continuously compounding one), eventually degrading effective warp tension and causing downstream defects (poor shed formation, uneven beating) — this is precisely why these two systems require SYNCHRONIZED, coordinated control relative to EACH OTHER (not just each being independently well-tuned in isolation), a systems-level insight distinct from simply ensuring each individual component''s settings are correct on their own.', 3, '{"letoff-takeup-synchronization"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
